  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>A small sandy bay tucked between rocks and Provençal villas, Graniers beach sits just past the marine cemetery, at the foot of Saint-Tropez's Citadel. It's one of the smallest public beaches in town (94 metres long), but also one of the most unspoiled — its setting feels remarkably natural despite being right next to the historic centre.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on the town's other beaches, the July 2026 municipal order allows dogs on leash from 6am to 9.30am and 6.30pm to 10pm, from 16 July to 15 September; banned the rest of the day during that period, with more relaxed rules off-season. This beach's listing on the tourist office's own site doesn't mention any specific dog hours at all — absent more precise information, trust the signage posted on site on the day of your visit.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: come early morning — the beach is tiny and fills up fast in summer, and that's also the allowed window for your dog.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Saint-Tropez's historic centre and Citadel are a few minutes' walk away, as are the tiny <a href="/en/fiche-plages-centre-ville-saint-tropez">Ponche and Fontanette beaches</a>. <a href="/en/fiche-plage-canebiers-saint-tropez">Canebiers beach</a> lies a little further east.</p>
    </div>

